  "answer_text": "There are **three ways** to change a `<span>`'s color on mouse over. Here they are, from simplest to most flexible:\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 1: Pure CSS `:hover` (Recommended)\n\nThis is the simplest and most efficient approach. If you can identify an element by ID (`#`) or class (`.`), you can add the `:hover` selector to make changes only appear when the user mouses over an element.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <style>\n      span {\n        color: black;\n        cursor: pointer;\n      }\n\n      span:hover {\n        color: red;\n      }\n    </style>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <span>Hover over me!</span>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\n> You can also target a specific span by class (`span.myClass:hover`) or ID (`span#myId:hover`).\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 2: Inline JavaScript using `onmouseover` / `onmouseout`\n\nThe JavaScript code is attached inside quotes to an HTML tag using event handlers like `onmouseover` and `onmouseout`, which replace the CSS selector `:hover`.\n\nInside those quotes, translating CSS to JavaScript requires these conversions: use the keyword `this` to refer to the element the user is interacting with, add the property `.style.` onto the element, and convert hyphenated CSS attributes (e.g. `background-color`) to camelCase (e.g. `backgroundColor`).\n\n```html\n<span\n  onmouseover=\"this.style.color='red';\"\n  onmouseout=\"this.style.color='black';\"\n>\n  Hover over me!\n</span>\n```\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 3: External JavaScript using `addEventListener`\n\nAdding JavaScript inside HTML tags (inline) is inflexible and time-consuming. You can instead change styles of HTML elements in JavaScript at the top of your page or in a separate file.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head> </head>\n  <body>\n    <span id=\"mySpan\">Hover over me!</span>\n\n    <script>\n      const span = document.getElementById(\"mySpan\");\n\n      span.addEventListener(\"mouseover\", function () {\n        this.style.color = \"red\";\n      });\n\n      span.addEventListener(\"mouseout\", function () {\n        this.style.color = \"black\";\n      });\n    </script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udfc6 Which Method Should You Use?\n\n| Method | Best For |\n|---|---|\n| **CSS `:hover`** | Simple, clean styling \u2014 always the first choice |\n| **Inline JS** | Quick prototyping or dynamic values |\n| **External JS** | Complex logic, dynamic color changes, or working with many elements |\n\nFor most use cases, **pure CSS (Method 1)** is the best approach as it keeps styling separate from logic and requires the least code.",
